  "original_html": "<section aria-labelledby=\"primary-heading\" class=\"flex w-full flex-1 flex-col h-full max-w-[1024px]\"><h1 id=\"primary-heading\" class=\"sr-only\">Home</h1><div><div class=\"max-w-[680px] m-auto px-1 flex flex-col gap-y-6\"><h1 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\">They Say / I Say</h1><div class=\"border-b border-slate-200\"></div><div id=\"pm-1\" class=\"border-0 flex flex-col gap-y-4 ProseMirror\" data-context=\"doc\" data-input-id=\"pm-doc-html-fd49ca3e-1d8e-4348-b54b-db4450950308\" data-editable=\"false\" data-progress=\"\" contenteditable=\"false\" translate=\"yes\"><p><b>In this module, we focus on argument writing using short articles (1–2 pages) or excerpts.</b></p><p>With these texts, students practice reading and writing skills and discover what others are already saying about a topic. They continue to build habits of reading arguments by noticing claims, reasons, and evidence. Then with a bit of writing, students enter the conversation and practice the habits of writers as they find ideas and evidence worth responding to.</p><h2 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\" data-version=\"0\" id=\"h_-they-say-i-say\">🔎 They Say / I Say</h2><p>In <a href=\"https://wwnorton.com/search/dGhleSBzYXkgaSBzYXk=\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span><i>They Say / I Say: The Moves that Matter in Academic Writing,</i></span></a> Graff and Birkenstein describe academic writing as a “conversation” where students can frame their arguments as a response to what others have said <i>(They say)</i> and use templates to help construct their own response <i>(I say)</i>.</p><div class=\"grid place-items-center\" contenteditable=\"false\"><div class=\"relative flex flex-row justify-between group w-full\" id=\"image-view-7fdec504-7581-4379-97f9-ff69caf05434\" style=\"width: 100px;\"><a data-type=\"image-ref\" data-width=\"16.666666666666664\" class=\"self-center w-full\"><img src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/uploaded_file/1fd77b48-377d-4f56-bcae-853d41ceafa4/They_Say_I_Say_blue_.png\" class=\"w-full\"/></a></div></div><p><b>Graff and Birkenstein have this to say about engaging with the ideas of others:</b></p><p><i>One central goal of this book is to demystify academic writing by returning it to its social and conversational roots. Although writing may require some degree of quiet and solitude, the ‘they say / I say’ model shows students that they can best develop their arguments not just by looking inward but by doing what they often do in a good conversation with friends and family—by listening carefully to what others are saying and engaging with other views.</i></p><p><i>...Therefore, when it comes to constructing an argument (whether orally or in writing), we offer you the following advice: remember that you are entering a conversation and therefore need to start with ‘what others are saying,’ ...and then introduce your own ideas as a response.</i></p><h2 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\" data-version=\"0\" id=\"h_-explore-and-learn\">🧭 Explore and Learn</h2><p><b>One way to introduce students to the idea of having a conversation with a text is through annotation.</b></p><p>In this approach, students identify phrases or sentences in the text that that they are interested in responding to. They highlight or underline the words or phrases <i>(They Say)</i> and then write their own responses as annotations<i> (I Say)</i>. This early scaffolding through annotation helps students engage in a conversation with a single text.</p><p>After practicing this annotation approach, students are eventually ready to add in the skill of summarizing, paraphrasing, or quoting the “They Say” and writing more developed “I Say” responses.</p><p><b>Check out this slideshow</b> with examples of students trying out They Say / I Say with annotation and in response to an article:</p><iframe src=\"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/e/2PACX-1vQqvBMqeE2mdOjsvNKir0EQKHzWXaWY9wMOOMFlaS3d-qa-p-2gtgg00TZRVerwbqtl7ioGme_pimtg/embed?start=false&amp;loop=false&amp;delayms=3000\" height=\"475\" sandbox=\"allow-scripts allow-forms allow-same-origin allow-popups allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox\" title=\"Iframe for url: https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/e/2PACX-1vQqvBMqeE2mdOjsvNKir0EQKHzWXaWY9wMOOMFlaS3d-qa-p-2gtgg00TZRVerwbqtl7ioGme_pimtg/embed?start=false&amp;loop=false&amp;delayms=3000\" style=\"width: 100%;\" contenteditable=\"false\"></iframe><p><b>Next, look through this resource collection to discover more</b> about the templates from Graff and Berkenstein’s <i>They Say / I Say</i>. These templates, also called sentence starters, stems, or frames, can help students make deliberate choices about responding to texts and using sources in their own writing.</p><div class=\"grid place-items-center\" contenteditable=\"false\"><div class=\"relative flex flex-row justify-between group w-full\" id=\"image-view-b6937d8a-69d9-4384-8fe6-b4a3deaa5848\" style=\"width: 100px;\"><a data-type=\"image-ref\" data-width=\"16.071428571428573\" class=\"self-center w-full\"><img src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/collection/fc4ecba3-b382-4b1a-8960-b3b510cc62b4/image-1626113761-Screen_Shot_2021-07-09_at_5.07.19_PM.png\" class=\"w-full\"/></a></div></div><h2 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\" data-version=\"0\" id=\"h_they-say-i-say-resources\">They Say / I Say Resources</h2><div data-type=\"embed\" contenteditable=\"false\"><a href=\"https://library.hccs.edu/ld.php?content_id=65656719\" class=\"flex justify-between border border-slate-300\" target=\"_blank\"><div class=\"p-4 flex flex-col justify-between\"><p class=\"font-bold line-clamp-1\">TSIS Templates.Examples</p><p class=\"text-slate-600 line-clamp-2\"></p><p class=\"text-slate-400 text-sm\">library.hccs.edu</p></div><img class=\"h-32 w-32 border-l border-slate-300\" src=\"https://participate.community/docs/fd49ca3e-1d8e-4348-b54b-db4450950308/edit?guide=43c68faf-1f10-43eb-bac3-2a7d02bdf25c\"/></a></div><div data-type=\"embed\" contenteditable=\"false\"><a href=\"https://app.participate.com/pages/clone-of-making-claims-upper-elementary-are-bugs-the-perfect-meal\" class=\"flex justify-between border border-slate-300\" target=\"_blank\"><div class=\"p-4 flex flex-col justify-between\"><p class=\"font-bold line-clamp-1\">Making Claims Elementary ~ How to Choose the Perfect Pet</p><p class=\"text-slate-600 line-clamp-2\"></p><p class=\"text-slate-400 text-sm\">app.participate.com</p></div><img class=\"h-32 w-32 border-l border-slate-300\" src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/resource/f85e4130-c46d-4f74-b42a-5313c7950f30/hedgehog-468228_640.jpg\"/></a></div><div data-type=\"embed\" contenteditable=\"false\"><a href=\"https://www.csub.edu/eap-riap/theysay.pdf\" class=\"flex justify-between border border-slate-300\" target=\"_blank\"><div class=\"p-4 flex flex-col justify-between\"><p class=\"font-bold line-clamp-1\">Microsoft Word - they say horizontal 3.doc</p><p class=\"text-slate-600 line-clamp-2\"></p><p class=\"text-slate-400 text-sm\">www.csub.edu</p></div><img class=\"h-32 w-32 border-l border-slate-300\" src=\"https://participate.community/docs/fd49ca3e-1d8e-4348-b54b-db4450950308/edit?guide=43c68faf-1f10-43eb-bac3-2a7d02bdf25c\"/></a></div><div data-type=\"embed\" contenteditable=\"false\"><a href=\"https://davestuartjr.com/simple-argumentative-writing-template\" class=\"flex justify-between border border-slate-300\" target=\"_blank\"><div class=\"p-4 flex flex-col justify-between\"><p class=\"font-bold line-clamp-1\">A Simple, Two-Paragraph Template that Helps Kids to Really Argue - Dave Stuart Jr.</p><p class=\"text-slate-600 line-clamp-2\">In the last post, I shared the new argumentative focus I&#39;m experimenting with for the article of the week (AoW) assignment. Rather than choosing just any type of article, I&#39;m looking for articles that argue. [1] It&#39;s not exactly the discovery of the polio vaccine, but still, it&#39;s pretty cool. I like this new focus […]</p><p class=\"text-slate-400 text-sm\">davestuartjr.com</p></div><img class=\"h-32 w-32 border-l border-slate-300\" src=\"https://davestuartjr.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/11/stretching-cotton-argument-they-say-i-say.jpeg\"/></a></div><h2 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\" data-version=\"0\" id=\"h_-try-and-share\">💬 Try and Share</h2><h3 class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\" id=\"h_-try-and-share__h_choose-one-informational-article-below-and-join-the-conversation\">Choose one informational article below and join the conversation!</h3><p>Pick a topic that interests you and click on the article title to open it in a new window. Next, engage with the text using the “add comment” feature in Google Docs (click the comment symbol in the upper right-hand corner of the menu bar). Highlight words, phrases, or sentences from the article that you find interesting (<i>They Say</i>), then add your response using the “I Say” concept. Consider trying out some of the They Say / I Say templates from the resource collection above.</p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1GgIlo78BEEDBmvi5m1F4dNnrc_MaCWFk/view?usp=sharing\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>Is gaming good for kids?</span></a> (The Conversation)</p></li><li class=\"\"><p><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jJZIogdQaqAzhdoD19ZqiprstIOEKkj5/view?usp=sharing\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>Study: Instagram influencers tend to promote more unhealthy foods than other celebrities</span></a> (Science News for Students)</p></li><li class=\"\"><p><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1DF1wFBsAT0v4afbTU0UK5Iw93LdvRcqP/view?usp=sharing\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>Lying won’t stretch your nose, but it will steal some brainpower</span></a> (Science News for Students)</p></li><li class=\"\"><p><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1bhxkpzqn2ppwzxBjEB-aSKxGM-pwArIF/view?usp=sharing\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>Kids wrote pleas to help unwanted shelter pups find homes; it worked</span></a> (The Washington Post)</p></li><li class=\"\"><p><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jdK9bUcmmfV3mP7GYL-sSfx8oHKPX6HB/view?usp=sharing\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>After Texas school shooting, students across country walk out for gun law reform</span></a> (USA Today)</p></li><li class=\"\"><p><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1XQa8m3e0u_v-RXeTQ-CFzrhg6pERCy2Y/view?usp=sharing\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>Research offers mixed message on social media’s effects on adolescents</span></a> (Baltimore Sun)</p></li></ul><p>Be sure to comment on others’ responses to keep the conversation going. If you feel compelled, comment on more than one article!</p><h3 class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\" id=\"h_-try-and-share__h_-join-the-discussion\">💬 Join the Discussion</h3><div class=\"grid place-items-center\" contenteditable=\"false\"><div class=\"relative flex flex-row justify-between group w-full\" id=\"image-view-8803e310-f74f-45bd-8f09-93512e805bbf\" style=\"width: 231.25px;\"><a data-type=\"image-ref\" data-width=\"37.797619047619044\" class=\"self-center w-full\"><img src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/uploaded_file/76f13a11-ebca-4ac7-b59c-5281cf299faa/Discussion_icon_2022_copy_small.png\" class=\"w-full\"/></a></div></div><p><b>After trying out the annotation, join the discussion and write a reflection</b> about your experience with They Say / I Say:</p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p>What do you think about Graff and Birkenstein’s idea that engaging with a text is like entering a conversation?</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>How might your students benefit from using the They Say / I Say templates? What might be challenging for them</p></li></ul><h2 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\" data-version=\"0\" id=\"h_-earn-recognition\">🏅 Earn Recognition</h2><div data-type=\"badge-embed\" dat